1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,070
Energy gets wasted for a boring reason:

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:06,900
nobody trusts the forecast enough to act on it.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,078
In September 2026, I built Agentic Arbiter.

4
00:00:09,218 --> 00:00:11,900
It covers 238 real data-centre campuses across 36 US states.

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,074
Data centres run chillers even when

6
00:00:14,214 --> 00:00:16,400
the air outside is already cold enough.

7
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:22,900
It works hour by hour, deciding when it's safe to shut the chillers off.

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,730
It made no unsafe calls.

9
00:00:25,870 --> 00:00:28,400
The old controller made one.

10
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:32,400
Over five real years, that's about four hundred extra hours a year.

11
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:33,776
And it grades itself.

12
00:00:33,916 --> 00:00:37,684
Here it's missing its target, because the data was patchy during the hackathon window.

13
00:00:37,824 --> 00:00:38,900
It says so itself.

14
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,749
Second of 842 teams, out of more than four thousand people,

15
00:00:42,889 --> 00:00:44,824
at FortyGuard's hackathon, sponsored by NVIDIA.

16
00:00:44,964 --> 00:00:46,900
The UN's Cool Coalition featured it.

17
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,900
Next I want it running live on real sites, not just measured on them.

18
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:52,353
Then I looked at home.

19
00:00:52,493 --> 00:00:55,492
Pakistan has solar on millions of roofs, but the grid can't see it.

20
00:00:55,632 --> 00:00:58,400
Go over two hundred units a month, and every unit gets re-priced.

21
00:00:58,700 --> 00:00:59,660
I asked twenty-one families.

22
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,400
Seven of the ten with solar are already trying to use more sun by day.

23
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,553
So I'm building a Solar Home Agent.

24
00:01:05,693 --> 00:01:09,400
It reads tomorrow's weather, then picks when to run the motor and the iron.

25
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:13,400
Same habit: it remembers how wrong it's been.

26
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:16,478
Moving a few jobs into the afternoon keeps this home under the limit.

27
00:01:16,618 --> 00:01:17,900
About five thousand rupees this month.

28
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:19,872
Nearly nineteen thousand over the next six.

29
00:01:20,012 --> 00:01:22,400
It's a simulation, on one real month of Islamabad weather.

30
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:26,208
Thirteen said they'd only trust an app that explains itself.

31
00:01:26,348 --> 00:01:27,400
So mine does.

32
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,920
When it isn't sure, it says no,

33
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:32,900
and tells you why.

34
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:35,900
It keeps score against what the sun actually did.

35
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:38,969
But look what it can't fix: it still wastes most of its midday sun,

36
00:01:39,109 --> 00:01:39,900
and buys after dark.

37
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,700
Storage closes that.

38
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,402
Next: five to ten homes in Islamabad, over WhatsApp.

39
00:01:47,542 --> 00:01:51,700
Then add them into one neighbourhood forecast, with our distribution company.

40
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,162
The solar is already on Pakistan's roofs.

41
00:01:54,302 --> 00:01:57,700
I want to help the grid see it, and trust it.

